00:04:37.473 --> 00:04:43.913
Panos Panagiotopolis of Wageningen University, in a Fizz.org account of his PhD work, showed

00:04:43.913 --> 00:04:49.393
something most people get wrong about river barriers. A fish pass on a dam or weir is not

00:04:49.393 --> 00:04:51.613
enough for migrants to reach spawning water.

00:04:52.273 --> 00:04:57.433
Isolated ladders often fail when maintenance lapses, and they fail again when water flows

00:04:57.433 --> 00:05:00.333
are not coordinated across several sites on the same route.

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:05.733
Fish moving through those streams depend on consistent water levels, and on passages that

00:05:05.733 --> 00:05:08.413
stay clear as operators change daily settings.

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:14.213
Tracking across Dutch waterways found that fixes aimed at one structure at a time left many

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.853
fish unable to finish their routes.

00:05:15.853 --> 00:05:21.173
Making each barrier passable on paper still left the run incomplete when the structures

00:05:21.173 --> 00:05:25.993
were operated as separate machines. Maintenance crews have to clear debris on a regular

00:05:25.993 --> 00:05:30.833
cycle, and they have to adjust gate settings so the passes still work during peak migration.

00:05:31.553 --> 00:05:36.193
Passage rates improved measurably when water plants treated the whole river as one network

00:05:36.193 --> 00:05:41.453
instead of a string of independent weirs. Monitoring that pair's fish counters with real-time

00:05:41.453 --> 00:05:46.253
flow data, is the next tool name for spotting which of those adjustments produce the largest

00:05:46.253 --> 00:05:47.933
gains in completed migrations.

